    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Use $u=x^3$ to find the exact area bounded by $y=\\dfrac{x^2}{1+x^6}$, the $x$-axis, and $x=1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\text{ units}^2$",
      "worked": "The curve meets the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and is non-negative on $0\\leq x\\leq1$.\n\nLet $u=x^3$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{du}{dx}&=3x^2\\\\du&=3x^2\\,dx\\\\dx&=\\frac{du}{3x^2}\\end{aligned}$$\nWhen $x=0$, $u=0$; when $x=1$, $u=1$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}A&=\\int_0^1\\frac{x^2}{1+x^6}\\,dx\\\\&=\\frac13\\int_0^1\\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\,du\\\\&=\\frac13\\left[\\tan^{-1}u\\right]_0^1\\\\&=\\frac13\\left(\\frac\\pi4-0\\right)\\\\&=\\frac\\pi{12}\\text{ units}^2\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
  },
